[h1]Cabinets & Choices — v0.1 Initial Release[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.2.* | Multiplayer synchronized | Save-game compatible[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Minister Trait System[/h2]

Cabinet members accumulate traits through gameplay events. Traits reflect a minister's background, competencies, and political philosophy — and they interact with each other, creating emergent synergies and tensions.

[b]Core Cabinet Traits (24)[/b]
Tier 1 simple traits, Tier 2 kiss-curse trade-offs with real downsides, Tier 3 attribute-scaled triads covering integration, antagonism, exploration, and more.

[b]Age Traits (35)[/b]
Era-specific traits granted through historical period events — Renaissance humanists, Reformation theologians, Absolutist administrators, Revolutionary agitators.

[b]Conditional Traits (75)[/b]
Dynamic traits that spawn based on your realm's development level, societal value axes (17 ideological pairs), regional/religious context, military specialization, parliamentary activity, and specific game actions.

[b]Negative Traits (9)[/b]
Acquired through underperformance events; each has a dedicated rehabilitation chain that removes the trait when you address the underlying problem.

[i]Total: 143+ traits[/i]

[b]Notable traits:[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Iron Disciplinarian[/b] — Order, hierarchy, obedience — these are the tools of this advisor's trade.
[*] [b]Shadow Counselor[/b] — Operates in the spaces between official channels, weaving a web of informants and leverage.
[*] [b]Progressive Reformist[/b] — Burns with the conviction that the old ways must give way to the new.
[*] [b]War Hawk[/b] — Blood runs hot at the smell of gunpowder.
[*] [b]Master Integrator[/b] — Commands the full sweep of administrative, military, and diplomatic knowledge.
[*] [b]Grand Chancellor[/b] — Not a diplomat, but an architect of foreign policy.
[*] [b]Tribune of the People[/b] — A cabinet minister beloved by the commons is a rare and powerful thing.
[*] [b]Philosopher King[/b] — The dream of every idealist and the nightmare of every opportunist.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Synergy Events[/h2]

When ministers share complementary traits, synergy events fire during the yearly pulse and grant temporary country modifiers.

[list]
[*] [b]26 standard synergies[/b] — trait pair bonuses firing from the yearly pulse
[*] [b]10 dual-role synergies[/b] — cabinet × religious figure synergies on the same minister
[*] [b]12 cross-country synergy events[/b] — your ministers interact with neighboring courts
[/list]

Examples:
[list]
[*] [b]Court of Learning[/b] (Scholar + Learned Courtier): +8% research, +4% literacy, +8% institution growth
[*] [b]Iron Hegemony[/b] (Iron Disciplinarian + War Hawk): +5% discipline, -10% army costs, +10% antagonism
[*] [b]Shadow Network Activated[/b] (Shadow Counselor + Espionage Director): +20 power projection, +2 dip rep, +20% spy network
[*] [b]Caliphate Network[/b] (Caliphate Diplomat + Grand Vizier): +2 dip rep, -12% antagonism, +1 diplomatic capacity
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Minister Legacies[/h2]

Senior ministers can be recognized before they retire, granting them the [b]Retired Legend[/b] trait. When they die — or if they hold a legendary title (Grand Vizier, Tribune, Navigator, Philosopher King) — they leave a [b]permanent legacy modifier[/b] on your country. Legendary deaths grant stronger legacies than retirements.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Negative Traits & Rehabilitation[/h2]

Ministers who underperform acquire negative traits through yearly events. Each has a dedicated [b]rehabilitation chain[/b]: address the underlying problem (stabilize finances, reform institutions, remove estate privileges) and the negative trait is removed, leaving the minister stronger.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]New Subject Types (18)[/h2]

[b]Personal Union[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Junior Partner[/b] (Age 2) — retains its own ruler and independent foreign policy
[*] [b]Lesser Partner[/b] (Age 2) — the maturation of a Junior Partner after fifty years of shared dynasty
[/list]

[b]Shadow / Client[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Shadow State[/b] (Age 2) — formally independent, bound only by secret treaty
[*] [b]Client State[/b] (Age 3) — brought into a stronger power's orbit through treaty or released provinces
[*] [b]Puppet State[/b] (Age 5) — under full administrative supervision of its overlord
[/list]

[b]Elite & Cultural[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Elite Enclave[/b] (Age 2) — culturally kindred domain granted self-governance
[*] [b]Palatinate[/b] (Age 4) — an Elite Enclave grown to semi-sovereign power; can declare war and call levies
[*] [b]Artists' Commune[/b] (Age 2) — self-governing community of painters and craftsmen
[*] [b]Scientific College[/b] (Age 4) — scholarly institution free to pursue learning for its patron
[*] [b]Naval Administration[/b] (Age 3) — port town granted semi-independence to manage maritime logistics
[/list]

[b]Trade[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Associated Republic[/b] (Age 2) — independent republican state under formal patronage
[*] [b]Chartered Company[/b] (Age 5) — royally chartered trading corporation in distant lands
[/list]

[b]Governance[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Protectorate[/b] (Age 3) — weaker neighbor under protection without formal subjugation
[*] [b]Crown Dependency[/b] (Age 2) — realm bound by shared dynasty without a formal personal union
[*] [b]Holy Protectorate[/b] (Age 4) — coreligionist state under spiritual patronage
[*] [b]Provincial Governorate[/b] (Age 5) — high-autonomy region governed by a Crown-appointed magnate
[*] [b]Tax Farm[/b] (Age 5) — exploitative extraction arrangement with a powerful local lord
[*] [b]Military March[/b] (Age 5) — offensive border vassal with special military charter
[/list]

Some subject types require a matching estate privilege or government reform to unlock.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Era Advances (70)[/h2]

New advances spread across all five playable ages, unlocking subject types, estate privileges, government reforms, and regional flavor.

[list]
[*] [b]Age of Renaissance (6)[/b] — Royal Union Network, Shadow Diplomacy, Artistic Patronage, Noble Patronage, Republican Patronage, Dynastic Governance
[*] [b]Age of Discovery (15)[/b] — Client State Diplomacy, Naval Charter, Protectorate Rights, plus 12 cultural/regional flavor advances (Humanist, Islamic, Confucian, Italian)
[*] [b]Age of Reformation (14)[/b] — Scholarly Orders, Palatinate System, Holy Patronage, plus colonial doctrine and trade network advances
[*] [b]Age of Absolutism (21)[/b] — Puppet State Administration, Chartered Trading Companies, Provincial Governance, Tax Farming, Military Marches, plus religious settlement and confessional state advances
[*] [b]Age of Revolutions (14)[/b] — Late Absolutism, Enlightened Despotism, Reform from Above, Prussian Discipline, General Staff System, and more
[/list]

Many are mixed into existing age tech trees. New mini-trees (with branch-locked follow-on advances) unlock from the Age of Discovery onward.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]New Estate Privileges & Government Reforms[/h2]

[b]Estate Privileges (9)[/b] — Shadow Network, Patronage of the Arts, Noble Enclave Rights, Naval Charter, Scholarly Brotherhood, Royal Trading Charter, Governorate Charter, Tax Charter, March Charter

[b]Government Reforms (5)[/b] — Naval Administration, Colonial Charter System, Governorate System, Tax Farming Contracts, March Charter

